```
invalid.js:27:2 lint/style/useErrorCause ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

  i The original error is discarded because the `catch` clause has no parameter.
  
    25 │ 	doSomething();
    26 │ } catch {
  > 27 │ 	throw new TypeError("Something went wrong");
       │ 	^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    28 │ }
    29 │ 
  
  i Without a parameter, the code cannot access the original error, so its details are lost.
  
  i Add a parameter to the `catch` clause and pass it as the `cause` of the new error.
  

```

```
invalid.js:33:10 lint/style/useErrorCause ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

  i The error is destructured in the `catch` clause.
  
    31 │ try {
    32 │   throw new Error("Original error");
  > 33 │ } catch ({ message }) {
       │          ^^^^^^^^^^^
    34 │   throw new Error(message);
    35 │ }
  
  i Destructuring can lose information from the error object, such as the stack trace. The original error also cannot be passed as the `cause`.
  
  i Catch the error in a single variable, read its properties from that variable, and pass the variable as the `cause`.
  

```

